<Article slug="faceting">
  <p>
    A pot leaves the wheel round. Faceting is the choice to break that circle into planes: to cut
    flat faces into the wall so the form becomes something between a vessel and a cut stone.
  </p>

  <h2>Timing is everything</h2>
  <p>
    Faceting happens while the clay is still soft enough to cut cleanly but firm enough to hold
    its shape. Too wet and the wall slumps under the blade; too dry and it tears. Potters
    typically throw the wall a little thicker than usual to leave material for the cuts, then
    slice each facet in a single steady pass with a wire or a blade.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each cut is final. There is no sanding a facet back into place, which is why the rhythm of
    the planes, slightly irregular, a little different on every piece, is the mark of a hand
    rather than a machine.
  </p>

  <h2>Edges for the fire to find</h2>
  <p>
    Facets matter most in the kiln. Every edge is a place where glaze thins and breaks, revealing
    the clay beneath, and where flying soda vapour catches and builds. A faceted mug therefore
    shows more contrast than a smooth one: deep colour pooled in the flat faces, bright rust
    flashing along the ridges.
  </p>

  <h2>In the hand</h2>
  <p>
    Facets also change how a piece feels. A faceted cup sits more securely in the palm and gives
    the fingers somewhere to rest. Turn it slowly and the light moves from plane to plane, the
    same way it does across a well-cut stone.
  </p>
</Article>
